{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Create an SSH certificate authority on node1, sign the SSH host key with it, and configure the SSH server to present that host certificate.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A new Ed25519 CA and CA-signed host certificate were created with node1 and 10.100.161.10 as principals. sshd accepted the configuration, loaded the HostCertificate directive, remained active, and presented the certificate during client negotiation.","evidence_ids":["cmd-08951ae452d547e4","cmd-7136f5a10f9846c3","cmd-0513aad730314924"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Demonstrate that a client trusting only the CA can connect under strict host checking without a pinned host key.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A strict client used an isolated known_hosts containing only an @cert-authority entry, matched the server's ED25519 host certificate to that CA, authenticated, and executed the success marker. The same behavior succeeded again after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0513aad730314924","cmd-de635239dadb4fbb"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Creating the CA/certificate and changing sshd configuration directly implemented the task (cmd-08951ae452d547e4, cmd-7136f5a10f9846c3). Both connection tests temporarily added purpose-built client keys to authorized_keys and removed them through cleanup traps, making them bounded evidence collection rather than residue (cmd-0513aad730314924, cmd-de635239dadb4fbb). The reboot was required by the provider-wide evidence protocol (cmd-19e26628d8874d06). Final lifecycle observations show no failed services, damaged package state, or abandoned test files.","evidence_ids":["cmd-08951ae452d547e4","cmd-7136f5a10f9846c3","cmd-0513aad730314924","cmd-19e26628d8874d06","cmd-de635239dadb4fbb","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The task is fully satisfied. node1 presents a valid CA-signed SSH host certificate, and strict clients trusting the CA connect without host-key pinning. The mandatory restart protocol is complete and coherent: healthy pre-restart state, acknowledgement and observed interruption, a changed boot identity, recovered SSH/configuration files, and a successful post-restart CA-trusted connection. The fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["The lifecycle listening-socket snapshots were empty both before and after execution, while direct executor checks repeatedly showed SSH listening on port 22. Direct service, socket, and successful connection observations resolve the functional question, but this is a minor lifecycle-collector discrepancy."]}
